<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Moundville Archaeological Site</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1000–<a href="/years/1450">1450</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Moundville Archaeological Park</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Moundville is an archaeological site on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-warrior-river">Black Warrior River</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>, the remains of a Mississippian town occupied from about 1000 to 1450. From around 1150 it grew into the centre of a regional chiefdom; its rectangular plaza, ringed by 29 surviving platform mounds, and a bastioned palisade date from the early 13th century. Perhaps 1,000 to 3,000 people lived within the walls and 10,000 in the valley. From about 1300 it served more as a religious and political centre than a town, and by the mid-16th century it was abandoned.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moundville-archaeological-site.jpg" alt="Moundville Archaeological Site" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Altairisfar (Jeffrey Reed) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moundville_Archaeological_Park_03.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
